diff --git a/docs/services/monitoring.md b/docs/services/monitoring.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..9480e1ef
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/services/monitoring.md
@@ -0,0 +1,70 @@
+# Monitoring policy
+
+## Machine classification
+
+Cluster metadata exposes `machineType`: `vps` for managed containers, `physical`
+for other machines by default, and `vm` for aitherdev, em1 and build. Set an
+explicit value in the machine's `module.nix` when the default does not apply.
+
+Host exporter targets publish this as `machine_type` on the `infra`, `nodes`
+and `mon` jobs. This includes the local Prometheus/node-exporter pair, the peer
+monitor, and node ZFS/IPMI exporters. This value overrides a conflicting
+`monitoring.labels.machine_type`; other custom labels retain their precedence.
+Node targets also retain `type="node"` and their existing identity and role labels.
+
+## Filesystem notifications
+
+Critical filesystem alerts with all four labels `job="infra"`,
+`machine_type="vps"`, `alertclass="fsavail"` and `severity="critical"` reach
+email and Telegram, then terminate at the empty `blackhole` receiver before
+both SMS routes. The exception applies to every filesystem selected by the
+existing rules, including `/run`. `/run` is normally tmpfs; expanding a VPS
+disk does not expand it.
+
+Filesystem thresholds, five minute holds, hourly repeats and inhibition remain
+unchanged. VM, physical, node and monitor alerts retain their existing SMS
+eligibility, as do alerts missing the machine label and other VPS alert classes.
+
+## CPU usage alerts
+
+Staging (`stg`) and playground (`pgnd`) hypervisors share the relaxed CPU
+usage policy: warning above 80% and vpsAdminOS critical above 90%, both held
+for 50 minutes. Other locations, including a missing location label, keep the
+10 minute holds. Both policies require a boot age strictly above one hour.
+
+The relaxed alerts retain their actual location. Their names keep the
+`Staging` suffix for compatibility. CPU usage still averages the idle rate
+across CPU cores. Raw load-average, I/O-wait, storage CPU, filesystem and ZFS
+rules keep their existing policies.
+
+## Configuration changes
+
+Install the route on both Alertmanagers before updating either monitor, then
+update both monitors promptly. No exporter or node update is required. Changing
+target labels creates new series and alert identities and can reset pending
+alerts. During a partial rollout, an old unlabeled alert remains eligible for
+SMS; HA deduplication does not cover alerts with different label sets.
+
+Restoring the previous Alertmanager configuration restores SMS eligibility.
+Restoring the previous monitor configuration removes the machine type labels
+and returns playground CPU alerts to the strict policy. Existing
+Prometheus and Alertmanager state remains readable; no data migration or deletion
+is needed. Verify configuration reload health and representative target labels
+on both replicas after a configuration change.
+
+## Focused checks
+
+From the repository's pinned development shell:
+
+```sh
+nix build --no-write-lock-file --no-link \
+  .#checks.x86_64-linux.infra-monitoring-config \
+  .#checks.x86_64-linux.infra-monitoring-rules
+```
+
+The check evaluates real machine metadata, generated labels for both monitors,
+and offline Alertmanager routing with inert receivers. It also checks the
+filesystem rules with promtool. The CPU check covers thresholds, hold times,
+locations and boot suppression. Offline routing verifies receiver selection;
+it does not send notifications or exercise delivery, daytime activation,
+repeat timers or inhibition.
